Title: The Parting of Ways
Order: 8
Production Code: 1x08

FADE IN:

EXT. THOMAS JEFFERSON COLLEGE - CAPITOL AT WACO - MORNING (APRIL)

The session begins the same day on which the Congress of the United States has under consideration the resolutions, the adoption of which means war with Spain. The exterior is decorated with American flags and red, white and blue bunting. Passers-by comment on the patriotism of Jefferson College.

Enveloped in the decoration there is cloth of the color of mourning.

INT. THE IMPERIUM CHAMBER - MORNING

[[The Imperium Congress]] assembles. Huge weeping willows stand, one on each side of the speaker's desk. To the right of the desk, on an elevated platform, a group of women in widow's weeds: fifty, their husbands having been made the victims of mobs since the first day of January. To the left, huddled, one hundred children in tatters whose looks bespeak lives of hardship, robbed of their parents by the brutish cruelty of unthinking mobs.

Postmaster Cook, while alive, was a member of the Imperium. His seat is empty and draped in mourning. In it is a golden casket containing his heart, raked from the burning embers on the morning following the night of the murderous assault.

[[Berl Trout]], the Secretary of State of the Imperium, sits among the Chiefs.

Promptly at eleven o'clock Speaker [[Belton Piedmont]] takes the chair and raps for order. The chaplain prays, invoking the blessings of God upon the negro race at the most important crisis in its history. A committee crosses the campus to inform the president that Congress is in session awaiting his pleasure. According to custom the president comes in person to deliver his message.

[[Bernard Belgrave]] enters at the rear and marches forward. The Congress rises and stands with bowed heads as he passes through. The speaker's desk is moved back as a sign of the president's superior position. In the center of the platform he stands to speak, in a Prince Albert suit of finest black, a standing collar, a necktie snowy white. In his fierce, determined glance you can discover latent fires.

BERNARD
Two terrible and discordant sounds have burst forth upon the erstwhile quiet air and now fill your bosom with turbulent emotions. One is the blast of the bugle, fierce and loud, calling us to arms against a foreign nation to avenge the death of American seamen and to carry the cup of liberty to a people perishing for its healing draught. The other is the crackling of a burning house in the night's dead hours, the piteous cries of pain and terror from the lips of wounded babes; the despairing, heart-rending, maddening shrieks of the wife and mother; the harrowing groans of the dying husband and father, and the gladsome shout of the fiendish mob of white American citizens, who have wrought the havoc just described, a deed sufficiently horrible to make Satan blush and hell hastily hide her face in shame.

BERNARD (CONT'D)
I deem this, my fellow countrymen, as an appropriate time for us to consider what shall be our attitude, immediate and future, to this Anglo-Saxon race, which calls upon us to defend the fatherland and at the same moment treats us in a manner to make us execrate it. Seven million eyes are riveted upon you, hoping that you will be brave and wise enough to take such action as will fully atone for all the horrors of the past and secure for us every right due to all honorable, loyal, law-abiding citizens of the United States.

BERNARD (CONT'D)
When in 1619 our forefathers landed on the American shore, the music of welcome with which they were greeted was the clanking of iron chains ready to fetter them; the crack of the whip to be used to plow furrows in their backs; and the yelp of the blood-hound who was to bury his fangs deep into their flesh, in case they sought for liberty.

BERNARD (CONT'D)
The Emancipation Proclamation, as we all know, came not so much as a message of love for the slave as a message of love for the Union; its primary object was to save the Union, its incident, to liberate the slave. After all these years of unremitting toil, the negro was pushed out into the world without one morsel of food, one cent of money, one foot of land.

BERNARD (CONT'D)
Colored men are excluded from the jury box; colored lawyers are discriminated against at the bar. If a negro murders an Anglo-Saxon, however justifiably, let him tremble for his life if he is to be tried in our courts. On the other hand, if an Anglo-Saxon murders a negro in cold blood, without the slightest provocation, he will, if left to the pleasure of our courts, die of old age and go down to his grave in perfect peace.

BERNARD (CONT'D)
Since the close of the civil war no less than sixty thousand of our comrades, innocent of all crime, have been hurried to their graves by angry mobs, and to-day their widows and orphans and their own departed spirits cry out to you to avenge their wrongs.

BERNARD (CONT'D)
Fellow Countrymen, we must be free. The sun that bathes our land in light yet rises and sets upon a race of slaves. The question remaining before us, then, is, How we are to obtain this freedom? In olden times, revolutions were effected by the sword and spear. In modern times the ballot has been used for that purpose. But the ballot has been snatched from our hands. The modern implement of revolutions has been denied us. I need not say more. Your minds will lead you to the only gate left open.

BERNARD (CONT'D)
But this much I will say: let not so light, so common, so universal a thing as that which we call death be allowed to frighten you from the path that leads to true liberty and absolute equality. How glad am I that I can glance over this audience and see written upon your faces utter disdain of death.

BERNARD (CONT'D)
Let us then, at all hazards, strike a blow for freedom. If it calls for a Thermopylæ, be free. If it calls for a Valley Forge, be free. Gentlemen of the Imperium in Imperio, I await your pleasure.

He takes his seat. The house is as silent as a graveyard. All feel the time for words has passed and the next and only thing in order is a deed.

INT. THE IMPERIUM CHAMBER - LATER

Each man seems determined to keep his seat. At length one man, somewhat aged, rises.

AGED MEMBER
Fellow citizens, our condition is indeed past enduring and we must find a remedy. I have spent the major portion of my life in close study of this subject. My impression is that the negro will never leave this country. As long as we remain here as a separate and distinct race we shall continue to be oppressed. We must lose our identity. I, therefore, urge that we send the word forth that we amalgamate.

At the word "amalgamate" a storm of hisses and jeers drowns further speech and he crouches in his seat.

Another rises and advocates emigration to the African Congo Free State, great in area and rich in resources, in the hands of the weak kingdom of Belgium. It might be wrested from Belgium with the greatest ease; it might even be purchased, being the personal property of King Leopold. He urges it would be a terrible blow to the South: the proud Southerner would have his own forests to fell, the haughty Southern lady would at last be queen of her own kitchen. The suggestion meets with favor, but he does not make clear its practicability.

At length a bold speaker rises, courageous enough to stick a match to the powder magazine which Bernard has left uncovered in all their bosoms.

BOLD SPEAKER
I am for war!

It is cheered to the echo. He begins an impassioned invective against the South, recites horror after horror: hangings; a burning where a white woman struck the match; the Texas horror, when a colored man named Smith was tortured with a red hot poker, and his eyes gouged out, then slowly roasted to death. He has Mrs. Cook arise and gather her children about her and tell her sorrowful story.

The entire assembly breaks down in tears; men fall on each other's necks and weep like babes. Their breath comes quick with choking passion and there bursts from all their throats the one hoarse cry:

MEMBERS
War! war! war!

Bernard turns his head away and in his soul swears a terrible oath to avenge the wrongs of his people.

When quiet is restored the bold speaker offers resolutions.

BOLD SPEAKER (CONT'D)
Whereas, the history of our treatment by the Anglo-Saxon race is but the history of oppression, and whereas, our patient endurance of evil has not served to decrease this cruelty, but seems rather to increase it; and whereas, the ballot box, the means of peaceful revolution is denied us, therefore: Be it Resolved: That the hour for wreaking vengeance for our multiplied wrongs has come. Resolved secondly: That we at once proceed to war for the purpose of accomplishing the end just named, and for the further purpose of obtaining all our rights due us as men. Resolved thirdly: That no soldier of the Imperium leave the field of battle until the ends for which this war was inaugurated are fully achieved.

A dozen men are on their feet to move adoption. The motion is seconded and put.

CHAIRMAN
Are you ready to vote?

MEMBERS
Ready!

Belton, in the chair, says quietly:

BELTON
Not ready.

All eyes point eagerly and inquiringly to him. He calls the senior member to the chair and comes down upon the floor. He stands with massive, intellectual head thrown back and a look of determined defiance. He turns slowly, letting his eye sweep the entire circle of faces.

BELTON (CONT'D)
I call upon you all to bear me witness that I have ever in word and deed been zealous in the work of building up this Imperium, whose holy mission it is to grapple with our enemy and wrest from him our stolen rights, given to us by nature and nature's God. If there be one of you that knowest aught against my patriotism, I challenge him to declare it now; and if there be anything to even cast a suspicion upon me, I shall gladly court a traitor's ignoble doom.

He pauses. No one accepts the challenge.

BELTON (CONT'D)
By your silence I judge that my patriotism is above suspicion; and this question being settled, I shall feel free to speak all that is within me on the subject now before me. I have a word to say in defence of the south--

MEMBERS
No! No! No! No!

Friends crowd around him and beg him to desist: the current is so strong that it is death to all future usefulness to try to breast it.

BELTON (CONT'D)
On her soil I was born; on her bosom I was reared; into her arms I hope to fall in death; and I shall not from fear of losing popular favor desist from pointing out the natural sources from which her sins arise.

A MEMBER
Remember your scars!

BELTON (CONT'D)
Yes, I am scarred. I have been in the hands of an angry mob; I have dangled from a tree at the end of a rope; I have felt the murderous pistol drive cold lead into my flesh; I have been accounted dead and placed upon the dissecting table; I have felt the sharp surgical knife ripping my flesh apart when I was supposed to be dead; all of these hardships and more besides I have received at the hands of the South; but she has not and cannot drive truth from my bosom, and the truth shall I declare this day.

BELTON (CONT'D)
It was indeed an awful sin for the Anglo-Saxon to enslave the negro. But in judging a people we must judge them according to the age in which they lived, and the influence that surrounded them. If David were on earth alive to-day and the ruler of an enlightened kingdom, he would be impeached forthwith, fined for adultery, imprisoned for bigamy, and hanged for murder. Yet while not measuring up to the standard of morality of to-day, he was the man after God's own heart in his day and generation.

BELTON (CONT'D)
When slavery was introduced into America, it was the universal practice of mankind to enslave. Our President alluded to the fact that the negro was unpaid for all his years of toil. It is true that he was not paid in coin, but he received that from the Anglo-Saxons which far outweighs in value all the gold coin on earth. He received instruction in the arts of civilization, a knowledge of the English language, and a conception of the one true God and his Christ.

BELTON (CONT'D)
So, beloved fellow citizens, when we calmly survey the evil and the good that came to us through American slavery, it is my opinion that we find more good for which to thank God than we find evil for which to curse man.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Our race has furnished some brutes lower than the beasts of the field, who have stirred the passions of the Anglo-Saxon as nothing in all of human history has before stirred them. As for us, let us cease to furnish the inhuman brutes whose deeds suggest inhuman punishments.

BELTON (CONT'D)
In regard to the lynching of our Comrade Cook, I have this to say. Every feature connected with that crime but emphasizes its heinousness. Cook was a quiet, unassuming, gentlemanly being, enjoying the respect of all in a remarkable degree. Having wronged no one he was unconscious of having enemies. And the color of Cook's skin was the only crime laid at his door.

BELTON (CONT'D)
The South has defrauded us out of the ballot and she must restore it. But happily that day has passed and both races are thinking and are better prepared to vote. Let us carry this point and our flag will soon have all of our rights inscribed thereon. The struggle is on, and my beloved Congress, let me urge one thing upon you. Leave out revenge as one of the things at which to aim.

BELTON (CONT'D)
In His Holy Word our most high God has said: "Vengeance is mine." Great as is this Imperium, let it not mount God's throne and attempt by violence to rob Him of his prerogatives. In this battle, we want Him on our side and let us war as becometh men who fear and reverence Him.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Now, hear my solution of the race problem. The Anglo-Saxon does not yet know that we have caught the fire of liberty. He does not yet know that we have learned what a glorious thing it is to die for a principle, and especially when that principle is liberty. We must change the conception which the Anglo-Saxon has formed of our character. We should let him know that patience has a limit; that strength brings confidence; that faith in God will demand the exercise of our own right arm. Before we make a forward move, let us pull the veil from before the eyes of the Anglo-Saxon that he may see the New Negro standing before him humbly, but firmly demanding every right granted him by his maker and wrested from him by man.

BELTON (CONT'D)
One. Be it Resolved: That we no longer conceal from the Anglo-Saxon the fact that the Imperium exists, so that he may see that the love of liberty in our bosoms is strong enough to draw us together into this compact government.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Two. Resolved: That we earnestly strive to convince the Anglo-Saxon that we are now thoroughly wedded to the doctrine of Patrick Henry: "Give me liberty or give me death." Let us teach the Anglo-Saxon that we have arrived at the stage of development as a people where we prefer to die in honor rather than live in disgrace.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Three. Resolved: That we spend four years in endeavors to impress the Anglo-Saxon that he has a New Negro on his hands and must surrender what belongs to him. In case we fail by these means to secure our rights and privileges we shall all, at once, abandon our several homes in the various other states and emigrate in a body to the State of Texas, broad in domain, rich in soil and salubrious in climate. Having an unquestioned majority of votes we shall secure possession of the State government.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Four. Resolved: That when once lawfully in control of that great state we shall, every man, die in his shoes before we shall allow vicious frauds or unlawful force to pursue us there and rob us of our acknowledged right.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Five. Resolved: That we sojourn in the state of Texas, working out our destiny as a separate and distinct race in the United States of America.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Such is the proposition which I present. It is primarily pacific: yet it is firm and unyielding. It courts a peaceable adjustment, yet it does not shirk war, if war is forced. Our beloved President, in his message to this Congress, made a serious mistake when he stated that there were only two weapons to be used in accomplishing revolutions. He named the sword and ballot. There is a weapon mightier than either of these. I speak of the pen.

BELTON (CONT'D)
My speech is made, my proposition is before you. I have done my duty. Your destiny is in your own hands.

His propositions are adopted in their entirety without one dissenting voice. The waves of the sea are calm; Belton is at the helm. When the members leave the hall that evening they breathe freely, feeling that the great race problem is, at last, about to be definitely settled.

As Belton is leaving the chamber, Bernard puts his hands fondly on his shoulders. His curly hair is disordered, a strange fire gleams in his eye.

BERNARD
Come over to the mansion to-night. I wish much to see you. Come about nine P.M.

Belton agrees to go.

INT. THE PRESIDENT'S MANSION - NIGHT

At the hour appointed Belton is at the door and Bernard is there to meet him. They enter the same room where years before Belton had, in the name of the Congress, offered Bernard the Presidency. The evening is mild; the window, which runs down to the floor, is hoisted. The moon sheds her full light; Bernard has not lighted his lamp. They take seats near the window, one on one side and one on the other, their faces toward the lawn.

BERNARD
Belton, that was a masterly speech you made to-day. If orations are measured according to difficulties surmounted and results achieved, yours ought to rank as a masterpiece. Let me congratulate you.

He extends his hand and grasps Belton's feelingly.

BELTON
(melancholy)
Bernard, that speech and its result ended my life's work. I have known long since that a crisis between the two races would come some day and I lived with the hope of being used by God to turn the current the right way. This I have done, and my work is over.

BERNARD
Ah, no, Belton; greater achievements, by far, you shall accomplish. I have called you over here to-night to acquaint you with a scheme that means eternal glory and honor to us both. When you closed your speech to-day, a bright light shot athwart my brain and revealed to me something glorious. I came home determined to work it out in detail. This I have done, and now I hand this plan to you to ascertain your views and secure your cooperation.

He hands Belton a foolscap sheet.

INSERT - [[The Plan of Action]]

"A Plan of Action for the Imperium in Imperio."

"1. Reconsider our determination to make known the existence of our Imperium, and avoid all mention of an emigration to Texas."

"2. Quietly purchase all Texas land contiguous to states and territories of the Union. Build small commonplace huts on these lands and place rapid fire disappearing guns in fortifications dug beneath them. All of this is to be done secretly, the money to be raised by the issuance of bonds by the Imperium."

"3. Encourage all Negroes who can possibly do so to enter the United States Navy."

"4. Enter into secret negotiations with all of the foreign enemies of the United States, acquainting them of our military strength and men aboard the United States war ships."

"5. Secure an appropriation from Congress to hold a fair at Galveston, inviting the Governor of Texas to be present. It will afford an excuse for all Negro families to pour into Texas. It will also be an excuse for having the war ships of nations friendly to us, in the harbor for a rendezvous."

"6. While the Governor is away, let the troops proceed quietly to Austin, seize the capitol and hoist the flag of the Imperium."

"7. We can then, if need be, wreck the entire navy of the United States in a night; the United States will then be prostrate before us and our allies."

"8. We will demand the surrender of Texas and Louisiana to the Imperium. Texas, we will retain. Louisiana, we will cede to our foreign allies in return for their aid. Thus will the Negro have an empire of his own, fertile in soil, capable of sustaining a population of fifty million people."

Belton ceases reading and returns the paper.

BERNARD
What is your opinion of the matter, Belton?

BELTON
It is treason.

BERNARD
Are you in favor of it?

BELTON
No. I am not and never shall be. I am no traitor and never shall be one. Our Imperium was organized to secure our rights within the United States and we will make any sacrifice that can be named to attain that end. Our efforts have been to wash the flag free of all blots, not to rend it; to burnish every star in the cluster, but to pluck none out.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Candidly, Bernard, I love the Union and I love the South. Soaked as Old Glory is with my people's tears and stained as it is with their warm blood, I could die as my forefathers did, fighting for its honor and asking no greater boon than Old Glory for my shroud and native soil for my grave. I shall never give up my fight for freedom, but I shall never prove false to the flag. These are my unalterable opinions.

BERNARD
Well, Belton, we have at last arrived at a point of separation in our lives. I know the Anglo-Saxon race. He will never admit you to equality with him. I am fully determined on my course of action and will persevere.

Each knows further argument is unnecessary. They rise and stand looking each other squarely in the face. They shake hands in silence. Tears are in the eyes of both. Each feels he is heeding the call of duty, and neither has ever been known to falter.

Belton returns to his room and retires. Bernard calls his messenger and sends him for every man of prominence in the Congress.

INT. JEFFERSON COLLEGE - DORMITORY - NIGHT

They all sleep in the building. The leaders get out of bed and hurry to the president. He lays before them the plan he showed Belton. They all accept it and pronounce it good. He then tells them he submitted it to Belton but that Belton was opposed. They are taken somewhat by surprise and, finding Belton opposed, are sorry they spoke so hastily.

Bernard knows such will be their feelings. He produces a written agreement and asks all who favor the plan to sign it, as that will be of service in bringing over other members. Ashamed to appear vacillating, they sign.

INT. THE IMPERIUM CHAMBER - NEXT DAY

The Congress assembles. President Belgrave submits his plan. Belton sweeps the assembly with his eyes and tells at a glance that there is a secret, formidable combination, and decides it would be useless to oppose.

The President's plan is adopted. Belton alone votes no.

BELTON
Being no longer able to follow where the Imperium leads, I hereby tender my resignation as a member.

The members stand aghast: death alone removes a member from the ranks of the Imperium, and asking to resign, according to their law, is asking to be shot. Bernard and every member of the Congress crowd around Belton and beg him to reconsider, and not be so cruel to his comrades as to make them fire bullets into his noble heart.

Belton is obdurate. According to the law he is allowed thirty days in which to reconsider. Ordinarily those under sentence of death are kept in close confinement; not so Belton. He is allowed all liberty. It is the secret wish of every one that he might take advantage of it and escape. But Belton is resolved to die.

INT. TRAIN - NIGHT

As he feels his days on earth are few, his mind turns to Antoinette. He steals away to Richmond to have a last interview with her. All the pent up passion of years bursts forth, and as the train speeds toward Virginia he feels that love will run him mad ere he sees Antoinette once more.

EXT. RICHMOND - ANTOINETTE'S HOUSE - MORNING

Meanwhile: Antoinette Nermal Piedmont, who had been excluded from her church, yearned so much to be able to present to Belton proofs of her chastity; but there was that white child. As the child grew, its hair began to change. His skin grew darker by degrees. As his features developed he was seen to be the very image of Belton. At length the child became Antoinette's color, retaining Belton's features, and then a shade darker than his father.

The church has met and rescinded its action of years ago. Every social organization of standing has elected her an honorary member. She gently smiles but does not seek their company. She prays hourly for God to bring Belton back.

One morning as she sits on her front porch enjoying the morning breeze, she looks toward the gate and sees her husband entering. She screams loudly, rushes into her son's room and drags him out of bed, not allowing him time to dress, dragging him to the door.

Belton rushes into the house.

ANTOINETTE NERMAL
Belton! there is your white child! Look at him! Look at him!

The boy looks up at Belton, and if ever one person favored another, this child favors him. Belton is dazed. He looks from child to mother and from mother to child. By and by it begins to dawn on him that the child is somehow his child.

She rushes to her album and shows him pictures of the child at various stages of growth. Belton discerns the same features in each photograph, but a different shade of skin. His knees begin to tremble. He had come, as the most wronged of men, to grant pardon. He now finds himself the vilest of men, unfit for pardon.

BELTON
(hands before his face)
O, God, what crime is this with which my soul is stained?

She springs toward him, tears his hands from his face, kisses him passionately, begs him to kiss and embrace her once more.

BELTON (CONT'D)
Unworthy, unworthy.

Antoinette bursts into weeping.

THE PIEDMONT CHILD
Papa, why don't you kiss Mama?

Belton raises his eyes and, seeing his image, which Antoinette brought into the world, grasps her in his arms and covers her face with kisses.

INT. PIEDMONT HOUSE - DAYS

For days he is lost in contemplation of his new found happiness. But at last a frightful picture arises: he is doomed to die, and the day of his death comes galloping on. He looks upon his boy and feels that his son will fill his place in the world. Antoinette is so happy he cannot have the heart to tell her of his fate. In her happiness she freely forgives the world for all the wrongs it has perpetrated upon her.

EXT. RICHMOND - RAILWAY STATION - DAY

At length the day draws near for Belton to go to Waco. He takes a tender leave of his loved ones. It is so tender that Antoinette is troubled and presses him hard as to when he is to return or send for them. He begs her to be assured of his love and know that he will not stay away one second longer than necessary. Thus assured, she lets him go, after kissing him more than a hundred times.

Belton turns his back on this home of happiness and love to walk into the embrace of death.

EXT. JEFFERSON COLLEGE - THE KNOLL - DAWN

In one part of the campus a high knoll surrounded on all sides by trees. In the early morn while the grass yet glitters with pearls of water and the birds begin to chirp, Belton is led forth to die. The sun has not yet risen but has reddened the east.

Belton is stationed upon the knoll, his face toward the coming dawn, hands folded calmly across his bosom, gazing over the heads of the executioners at the rosy east.

His executioners, five in number, stand facing him twenty paces away. Berl Trout is one of the five. They are commanded by Bernard, the President of the Imperium, who gazes on Belton with eyes of love and admiration. He loves his friend but he loves his people more. Viola has taught him that lesson. His eyes swim with tears.

BERNARD
(hoarse whisper)
Belton Piedmont, your last hour has come. Have you anything to say?

BELTON
(firm, ringing tones that startle the birds into silence)
Tell posterity that I loved the race to which I belonged and the flag that floated over me; and, being unable to see these objects of my love engage in mortal combat, I went to my God, and now look down upon both from my home in the skies to bless them with my spirit.

Bernard gives the word of command to fire. Belton falls forward, a corpse.

EXT. JEFFERSON COLLEGE - THE KNOLL - LATER

On the knoll where he fell he is buried, shrouded in an American flag.

BERL TROUT (V.O.)
I was a member of the Imperium that ordered Belton to be slain. It fell to my lot to be one of the five who fired the fatal shots and I saw him fall. Oh! that I could have died in his stead!

BERL TROUT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
When he fell, the spirit of conservatism in the Negro race, fell with him. His influence, which alone had just snatched us from the edge of the precipice of internecine war, was now gone; his restraining hand was to be felt no more.

Bernard stands by the side of Belton's grave and sees the stiffened form of his dearest friend lowered to its last resting place. His grief is of a kind too galling for tears. He laughs a fearful, wicked laugh like unto that of a maniac.

BERNARD
Float on proud flag, while yet you may. Rejoice, oh! ye Anglo-Saxons, yet a little while. Make my father ashamed to own me, his lawful son; call me a bastard child; look upon my pure mother as a harlot; laugh at Viola in the grave of a self-murderer; exhume Belton's body if you like and tear your flag from around him to keep him from polluting it! Yes, stuff your vile stomachs full of all these horrors. You shall be richer food for the buzzards to whom I have solemnly vowed to give your flesh.

BERL TROUT (V.O.)
These words struck terror to my soul. With Belton gone and this man at our head, our well-organized, thoroughly equipped Imperium was a serious menace to the peace of the world. A chance spark might at any time cause a conflagration, which, unchecked, would spread destruction, devastation and death all around.

BERL TROUT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I was determined to remove the possibility of such a catastrophe. I decided to prove traitor and reveal the existence of the Imperium that it might be broken up or watched. My deed may appear to be the act of a vile wretch, but it is done in the name of humanity. Long ere you shall have come to this line, I shall have met the fate of a traitor. I die for mankind, for humanity, for civilization.

BERL TROUT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
If the voice of a poor Negro, who thus gives his life, will be heard, I only ask as a return that all mankind will join hands and help my poor down-trodden people to secure those rights for which they organized the Imperium, which my betrayal has now destroyed.

EXT. THOMAS JEFFERSON COLLEGE - TOWER - SUNSET

The campus. The tower. The knoll.

BERL TROUT (V.O.)
When will all races and classes of men learn that men made in the image of God will not be the slaves of another image?

FADE OUT.

THE END
